WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:01.320
Skills are awesome.

2
00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:02.880
But there's an irony to them.

3
00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.120
They're built to be reusable,

4
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.120
but they don't actually have a distribution system.

5
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.000
So what people end up doing is they're copying down these markdown

6
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.480
files and folders, and they're checking them into their repos

7
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.120
and sometimes even multiple times within the same repo,

8
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.360
because they want different agents to consume them.

9
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.400
Ah, that’s the worst, when there's so much sprawl across a number of different

10
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.560
repos, there's, you know, stale skills hanging around, not getting updates.

11
00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.400
It's terrible what you're essentially saying is

12
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.000
we need a kind of package manager to organize this. Precisely.

13
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.800
You know, we've solved this problem in software.

14
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.480
We know that we can package them up. We can version them.

15
00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.240
We can build manifest files that track what you have installed.

16
00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:36.920
These are solved problems.

17
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:38.680
We just haven't solved them from skills.

18
00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.280
But what did Tessl release?

19
00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:41.440
How can Tessl help with this problem?

20
00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.480
So Tessl launched a package manager for skills and other types of context.

21
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.640
We help you install skills.

22
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.160
You can install any skill from any GitHub

23
00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:52.160
repo and such, but it will install it via the registry.

24
00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.560
And we will keep a manifest file that remembers what you have installed.

25
00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.520
And it gives you actions like, you know, updating them over time.

26
00:00:58.520 --> 00:00:59.600
You can track them.

27
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.320
It doesn't duplicate content in your system,

28
00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.360
just like a proper professional developer grade package manager would do.

29
00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.240
So I'm a heavy skills user today.

30
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:10.040
How can I use Tessl to to discover new skills,

31
00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.040
but also use my existing skills that I have in my system today?

32
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.880
So to consume skills you just install the Tessl CLI from npm.

33
00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.880
And you can run Tessl install of any skill or Tessl skill search

34
00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:21.080
if you want to find others.

35
00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:23.400
And once you've done it, you'll have a manifest file.

36
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.280
It'll be there.

37
00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:26.640
You can continue to action it.

38
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.120
You can also check out the registry to find the evaluations

39
00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.960
of these different skills so you can pick the better ones.

40
00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:36.360
If you are creating skills, then you can publish skills

41
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:40.240
to the registry through again CLI actions that should feel very natural.

42
00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.960
And then you can use those and manage them in workspaces

43
00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.560
to distribute them across your team with the right access controls.

44
00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:45.960
Amazing.

45
00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.160
So if I'm a publisher, I can actually create and share skills

46
00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:52.840
for myself, others in the community as well as in my organization.

47
00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:53.360
Precisely.

48
00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.000
And it will even run those evaluations for you when you publish.

49
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:56.360
Amazing.

50
00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:59.360
So now I know where my skills are and also whether they're any good or not.

51
00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:00.160
Precisely. Amazing.

52
00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:01.320
So if you are using skills

53
00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.720
or your producing skills, head over to tessl.io/registry,

54
00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.720
where you can publish skills that you and your organization use,

55
00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.000
as well as discover

56
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.560
other skills from the community that you can download and use today.
